---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/human-body-systems">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Biology. It needs zero prior knowledge. Its
    starting point is your own wrist: press two fingers there and feel the
    beat. Something inside you is pumping, right now, and this lesson is
    about what that pump does, the pipes it drives, and the cargo they
    carry.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what the circulatory system is for, and why the body needs a delivery service at all;</li>
    <li>trace a drop of blood through the heart and both of its loops;</li>
    <li>name the three types of blood vessel and match each to its job;</li>
    <li>list what blood is made of, and what its main cells do;</li>
    <li>explain homeostasis with one worked example, such as blood sugar.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with your own pulse</h2>
  <p>
    The human body is <strong>eleven interlocking systems built from about 37
    trillion cells</strong>, each system a specialist service for the whole.
    Most of those cells sit far from any source of food or oxygen — a cell
    in your calf is a metre from the air you breathe — so something must
    deliver to them constantly, or they die within minutes. That something
    is the <strong>circulatory system</strong>, and it is this lesson's
    spine: the body's transport network, from the pump (the heart) to the
    pipes (the vessels) to the cargo (the blood).
  </p>
  <p>
    One theme runs through all eleven systems: <strong>homeostasis</strong>
    — keeping temperature, pH, sugar and salt inside narrow ranges despite a
    chaotic world. Health is homeostasis holding. Disease is it slipping.
    Medicine is mostly helping it back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Circulation is the clearest place to watch homeostasis working, because
    blood is the medium every other system's output travels in — hormones
    from the endocrine system, heat from muscles, glucose from digestion,
    antibodies from the immune system. Read the transport system well and
    you have read the body's operating manual. The widget below groups all
    eleven systems by what they do for the whole.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the systems, grouped by what they do for the whole" widget="equationStep" config={bodyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click through the four groups and name
    a member of each — the grouping is by job done for the whole body, not
    by textbook order. Notice that the delivery service comes first,
    because every other group's output — hormones, heat, glucose,
    antibodies — travels in the blood. And note the last card's closing
    point: running down a street needs five systems in the same second.
  </p>

  <h2>Circulatory systems: the design space</h2>
  <p>
    Simple animals skip the problem: a flatworm is thin enough that
    diffusion alone feeds every cell. Anything thicker needs a dedicated
    fluid, driven in bulk — a <strong>circulatory system</strong>. Two
    design decisions define all of them. The first is <strong>open versus
    closed</strong>. In an open system (insects, most molluscs) the blood —
    called haemolymph — spills from vessels into a body cavity and sloshes
    around the organs before being drawn back; it is cheap and fine for low
    demands. In a closed system (vertebrates, earthworms, squid) the blood
    stays inside vessels throughout, held at high pressure and routed
    precisely — the design that supports big, active bodies.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second decision is <strong>single versus double
    circulation</strong>. Fish have a single loop: heart &rarr; gills
    &rarr; body &rarr; heart — one pump, and blood loses pressure while
    picking up oxygen, which is part of why fish are limited to water.
    Birds and mammals run a <strong>double circulation</strong>: the right
    heart drives a low-pressure loop to the lungs, the left heart a
    high-pressure loop to the body — two pumps in series, so the body loop
    never suffers the pressure drop of the lung stopover. That split is
    what makes mammalian-sized, warm-blooded, high-metabolism life
    possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the heart pumps blood around and that's one loop."
    Trace a red cell: right atrium &rarr; right ventricle &rarr; lungs &rarr; left atrium &rarr; left
    ventricle &rarr; body &rarr; back to the right atrium. It passes through the heart <em>twice</em>
    per lap, and the two halves are pumping into separate circuits — which is why the
    left ventricle's wall is about 3× thicker than the right's.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="trace the drop of blood — one full lap" widget="order" config={hbTraceOrder} />

  <h2>The heart: a self-wiring double pump</h2>
  <p>
    The heart is a fist of cardiac muscle with four chambers and four
    one-way valves. <strong>Atria</strong> receive; <strong>ventricles</strong>
    eject. The tricuspid and mitral valves guard the atrium–ventricle
    doors; the pulmonic and aortic valves guard the exits. The beat runs on
    its own built-in electrical system: the <strong>sinoatrial node</strong>
    — a patch of self-firing cells in the right atrium — sparks about every
    0.8 s at rest, the wave sweeps the atria, and the
    <strong>atrioventricular node</strong> relays it to the ventricles after
    a built-in delay, so the upper chambers finish emptying first.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each part with its role in the beat" widget="match" config={hbHeartMatch} />
  <p>
    "Lub-dub" is the sound of valves slamming: first the
    atrioventricular pair as the ventricles contract
    (<strong>systole</strong>), then the outlet valves as they refill
    (<strong>diastole</strong>). Output is a product of two numbers:
    <strong>cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume</strong> — about 72
    beats per minute × 70 mL ≈ 5 L per minute at rest, and up to 20–30 L
    per minute in trained athletes, most of the extra coming from a bigger
    stroke volume: exercise empties the ventricles harder and fills them
    deeper.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="call the magnitude — resting cardiac output" widget="estimate" config={hbOutputEstimate} />
  <p>
    The heart feeds itself first. <strong>Coronary arteries</strong> branch
    off the aorta's very first centimetre, because the muscle cannot borrow
    from the blood it pumps through its own interior chambers. Block them
    — <strong>atherosclerosis</strong>, the gradual furring of arteries
    with fatty plaque — and the muscle downstream starves: angina when
    partial, a heart attack (myocardial infarction) when complete. It
    remains the world's leading cause of death, and its big four risk
    factors — smoking, high blood pressure, bad lipids, inactivity — are
    the modifiable ones.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the heart is a self-timing, four-chambered
    double pump that feeds itself through coronary arteries — and blocking
    those arteries is the modern world's biggest killer. Next: the pipes
    the pump drives.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood vessels: three pipes, three jobs</h2>
  <p>
    Vessels are a study in structure matching function.
    <strong>Arteries</strong> carry blood away from the heart at high
    pressure: thick, elastic walls stretch with each pulse and recoil
    between beats, smoothing intermittent ejection into continuous flow —
    feel your pulse; that is the artery wall recoiling.
    <strong>Arterioles</strong> are the adjustable taps whose smooth-muscle
    rings route blood — to skin for cooling, to the gut after lunch, to
    the muscles in a sprint.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Capillaries</strong> are the destination: one endothelial cell
    thick, with a lumen barely wider than a red cell, so flow slows to
    single file and exchange has time to happen — every other vessel exists
    to feed these. <strong>Veins</strong> return at low pressure: thin
    walls, large capacity, and a problem — how does blood get uphill from
    your feet? Valves prevent backflow while your skeletal muscles squeeze
    the veins (the "muscle pump"; this is why standing still for hours
    pools blood in your legs, and why long flights raise clot risk).
    Varicose veins are failed valves; deep-vein thrombosis is a clot where
    you can least afford one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Blood pressure</strong> reads as systole-over-diastole, with
    120/80 mmHg the classic resting reference. Chronic hypertension is the
    "silent" risk factor because the suffering is deferred — the heart
    thickens, arteries stiffen, and the small vessels of kidneys, retina
    and brain give out first.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a closed double circulation carries blood from
    a four-chambered pump through arteries, capillaries and veins. Next:
    the cargo itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood: the cargo and the crew</h2>
  <p>
    Blood is a tissue: about 55% <strong>plasma</strong> (water, proteins,
    glucose, hormones, antibodies, wastes) and about 45% cells.
    <strong>Red cells</strong> (erythrocytes) are concave discs, released
    from the marrow without a nucleus, each packing about 270 million
    haemoglobin molecules — oxygen taxis that bind O<sub>2</sub> in the
    lungs and release it where levels drop. Each lives about 120 days; the
    marrow replaces about 2 million per second. <strong>White cells</strong>
    (leucocytes) are the immune crew — neutrophils as fast-response
    bacteria hunters, lymphocytes as the targeted intelligence service
    behind antibodies and vaccination.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Platelets</strong> are cell fragments that trigger clotting: a
    cascade that turns an exposed vessel wall into a fibrin plug within
    minutes — life-saving at a cut, lethal inside a coronary artery. Blood
    groups (the ABO and rhesus systems) are inherited surface-sugar
    variants; transfuse the wrong one and the recipient's antibodies
    destroy the gift — which is why a donated unit is typed, cross-matched
    and traced from vein to vein.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — the transfusion answer" widget="spot-error" config={hbGroupSpot} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "blood is blue inside the body and turns red on
    contact with air." Oxygen-poor blood is dark red, never blue; veins look bluish
    through skin because of how light scatters in tissue and how the eye reads colour
    through the layers. The blue vein diagram is a colouring convention, not physiology
    — and no blood bank has ever drawn a blue bag.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — the colour of blood" widget="judge" config={hbColourJudge} />

  <h2>The other systems, and homeostasis holding</h2>
  <p>
    Two homeostasis loops show how the other systems talk to the blood.
    <strong>Thermoregulation:</strong> the hypothalamus is the thermostat —
    sweat when hot (evaporative cooling), shiver when cold
    (muscle-generated heat), skin vessels flush or clamp — holding core
    temperature within about 1 °C of 37 °C. <strong>Blood sugar:</strong>
    after a meal the pancreas releases <strong>insulin</strong>, which
    tells cells to take up glucose and the liver to store it; between
    meals <strong>glucagon</strong> tells the liver to release it. Two
    hormones in a push–pull loop keep blood glucose near 5 mmol/L;
    <strong>diabetes</strong> is this loop failing (type 1: the
    insulin-producing cells are destroyed; type 2: the targets stop
    answering), and injected insulin — isolated in 1921 — remains one of
    medicine's great saves.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="the thermostat's two moves — drag from hot to cold" widget="compare" config={hbThermoCompare} />
  <p>
    Note the pattern in both loops: <strong>negative feedback</strong>
    (output cancels the deviation) stabilises, which is most of
    physiology. <strong>Positive feedback</strong> drives rare events to
    completion — childbirth amplifies its own contractions, clotting
    amplifies itself — because an event, not a level, is wanted.
  </p>
  <p>
    The skin — the integumentary system — deserves a sentence as
    homeostasis's largest organ: about two square metres of layered tissue
    doing temperature control (sweat glands and vessel dilation),
    waterproofing, immune screening and vitamin-D synthesis in one sheet.
    It is also the body's most conspicuous feedback display: flushed,
    pale, sweaty or clammy, the skin shows the thermostat's decisions in
    real time, which is why clinicians read it before any monitor is
    attached.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the stomach is where food is absorbed." The stomach's
    job is acid-and-enzyme demolition and sterilisation; the overwhelming majority of
    nutrient absorption — approaching 90% — happens along the small intestine's metres of
    villi and microvilli, whose folded surface exists precisely to be the absorptive
    stage, with the large intestine reclaiming water and salts. If absorption were the
    stomach's job, the body would have had no reason to build the small intestine's
    architecture at all.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="route the meal — who does which job?" widget="branch" config={hbAbsorbBranch} />
  <p>
    And the body's residents outnumber its cells: about 38 trillion bacteria
    live on and in you (the microbiome), aiding digestion, synthesising
    vitamins, training the immune system — the "you" being kept alive is an
    ecosystem.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does a thick animal need a circulatory system when a flatworm does not?</li>
    <li>Which chamber has the thickest wall, and what does that tell you about its job?</li>
    <li>Name the three vessel types and one feature each that fits its job.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Diffusion alone only feeds cells a
    fraction of a millimetre away; anything thicker needs fluid pumped in
    bulk. (2) The left ventricle — about 3× thicker than the right —
    because it drives blood at high pressure around the whole body, not
    just the short lung loop. (3) Arteries: thick elastic walls for high
    pressure. Capillaries: walls one cell thick for fast exchange. Veins:
    valves, because their pressure is low and blood may travel uphill.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The body is eleven interlocking systems built on one theme: homeostasis — holding temperature, pH, sugar and salt in narrow ranges.</li>
    <li>Cells need a delivery service because most sit far from any source of food or oxygen; diffusion alone serves only very thin animals.</li>
    <li>Birds and mammals run a double circulation — a low-pressure lung loop and a high-pressure body loop — driven by a four-chambered self-timing pump.</li>
    <li>Arteries carry blood away under high pressure, capillaries are one-cell-thick exchange surfaces, and veins return it through valves.</li>
    <li>Blood is plasma plus red cells (oxygen taxis), white cells (defence) and platelets (clotting).</li>
    <li>Insulin and glucagon run a push–pull loop that holds blood glucose near 5 mmol/L — negative feedback is most of physiology.</li>
    <li>Absorption happens along the small intestine, not the stomach, and the skin is homeostasis's largest organ.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The systems here run on chemistry taught elsewhere in the course.
    Movement into and out of cells explains how the capillary's cargo
    actually crosses membranes, biological molecules names what digestion
    dismantles food into, and photosynthesis and respiration are the two
    reactions the blood's cargo exists to serve. DNA, genes and heredity
    sits behind the blood groups and inherited conditions met along the way.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, the transport system is built with headroom. Blood
    volume is about 5 L and a full circuit takes about a minute at rest
    (the lung loop about 10 seconds), so the same litre of blood makes
    about 1,400 circuits a day — roughly 7,300 L moved, pushed by a pump
    that consumes about 5% of the body's energy budget for a lifetime of
    about 2.5–3 billion beats. Oxygen delivery is cardiac output ×
    haemoglobin content × saturation; at rest it roughly quadruples actual
    demand, and the reserve is what exercise draws on — VO<sub>2</sub>max,
    the ceiling, is the strongest single predictor of all-cause mortality
    in cohort studies. The adaptations run both ways: endurance training
    grows stroke volume (and thickens the left ventricle's <em>cavity</em>,
    unlike the wall-thickening of hypertension), and detraining reverses
    it within weeks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Comparative physiology sharpens the design argument. A giraffe stands
    about 5 m tall, so its brain sits about 2 m above its heart, demanding
    a blood pressure near 280/180 mmHg at the pump — held in check by
    tight leg-fascia and one-way valves against oedema, a natural
    experiment in hypertension management. Diving mammals (seals, whales)
    run reflex <em>diving bradycardia</em> — heart rates crashing from
    about 100 to under 10 beats per minute, blood shunted to brain and
    heart alone — the same selective-routing machinery your arterioles
    use, taken to its limit. And the transplant frontier shows what the
    remaining margin is: hearts can be cooled and stopped for a few hours
    (organ preservation), but a fully artificial replacement that beats 3
    billion times without clotting or wearing remains unsolved
    engineering — the biological pump still sets the standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    A third loop deserves a place because it couples the transport system
    to the excretory one: <strong>osmoregulation</strong>. The blood
    arriving at the kidneys is monitored for water and salt content; too
    concentrated, and the pituitary releases <strong>ADH</strong>, which
    makes the kidney tubules reabsorb more water — dark, scanty urine on a
    dehydrated day. Too dilute, and ADH falls — pale, copious urine after
    a large drink. The loop is negative feedback like blood sugar's, but
    note its speed and its seat: the correction runs through the same
    bloodstream the transport system moves, and the whole fluid volume of
    the body is retuned within hours. The kidney filters about 180 L of
    blood daily to reclaim about 178.5 L of it — transport at its most
    profligate and precise, and the reason the "waste disposal" system is
    really a homeostasis organ that happens to produce urine.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "everyone needs eight glasses of water a day." Thirst
    and the ADH loop <em>are</em> the regulation; needs vary enormously with body size,
    climate and activity, food supplies roughly a fifth of daily water, and ordinary
    caffeinated drinks still hydrate. The kidney, not a slogan, keeps the water balance
    — which is why the honest answer to "how much should I drink?" is "enough that your
    body stops asking."
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="file the signs by the water loop's direction" widget="sort-buckets" config={hbWaterBuckets} />
